<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Savva Morozov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1862">1862</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Savva Timofeyevich Morozov</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Savva Timofeyevich Morozov was a Russian textile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> and philanthropist of an Old Believer merchant family, the fifth-richest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> at the beginning of the 20th century. He studied physics and mathematics at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> University and chemistry at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, where he studied the British textile industry. As the largest shareholder of the Moscow Art <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> he funded the improvement of its building on Kamergersky Lane in 1902 and its electrification in 1903. Influenced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gorky">Maxim Gorky</a>, he funded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a>, including the newspaper Iskra. He died of a gunshot wound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> in 1905; his death was ruled a suicide, though various murder theories exist.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/savva-morozov.jpg" alt="Savva Morozov" width="330" height="514" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Морозов_Савва_Тимофеевич_(1862-1905)_;_(eng.-Morozov_Savva_Timofeevich).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Savva Morozov was the son of Timofei Savvich Morozov and Maria Feodorovna Morozova. The family fortune had been founded by Savva Vasilyevich Morozov (1770–1862), and the family held the hereditary rank of honorary citizens, which freed its members from conscription, corporal punishment and the poll tax. He grew up in the Morozov house on Trehsvyatitelskaya Lane on Ivanovo Hill in the White City of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, the most expensive home in the city, whose gardens were frequented by Sergey Aksakov, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fyodor-dostoevsky">Dostoevsky</a>, Ostrovsky, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Tolstoy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Tchaikovsky</a>.</p><p>He attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gymnasium">gymnasium</a> at Pokrovsky Gates. At Moscow University he wrote a study on dye and met Mendeleev. In January 1885 workers at the Morozov factories in Bogorodsk, especially Orekhovo-Zuyevo, went on strike for several weeks in what became known as the Morozov strike. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> he studied the structure of the textile industry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, above all in Manchester.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Morozov married Zinaida Grigorievna, née Zimin, the former wife of his second cousin. The couple held lavish parties and balls attended by Savva Mamontov, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodor-chaliapin">Feodor Chaliapin</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maxim-gorky">Maxim Gorky</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-chekhov">Anton Chekhov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstantin-stanislavski">Konstantin Stanislavski</a> and others; the actress Olga Knipper recalled never having seen such luxury and wealth.</p><p>The improvements he paid for on Kamergersky Lane were Schechtel's and included Anna Golubkina's plaster high relief The Wave above the right entrance. In 1903 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> gained its own power station and a second small stage, separate from the main building, so that full rehearsals could go on during performances. The new hall seated 1,200, a third more than before, and Morozov paid all its costs for the 1902–1904 seasons. When the rent rose for 1904–1905 he stopped paying the lease and running costs. After Stanislavski and Nemirovich-Danchenko received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Gorky</a>'s play Summerfolk poorly, Gorky left the theatre and Morozov followed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>With his relative Nikolai Pavlovich Schmidt, Morozov was a significant financial contributor to the Bolshevik faction of the Russian Social Democratic Workers Party. According to the author Suzanne Massie, he proposed to his mother, the family's matriarch, that the factories share their profits with the workers, one of the first industrialists to suggest the idea. In her account his mother angrily removed him from the family business, and a month later the apparently despondent Morozov shot himself in the south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a> on 26 May 1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Savva Morozov” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Anyone who could declare enough capital and pay the fees could enrol in a guild, and some merchants began as townspeople or even serfs: <b>Savva Morozov</b>, a serf, bought freedom for himself and his sons in 1820, and the family went on to found the Morozov textile companies.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Orekhovo-Zuyevo, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannes">Cannes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=55.8,38.97&amp;year=1889" aria-label="Orekhovo-Zuyevo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5399 262 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Savva Morozov"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5849 532h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5849 532h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=55.8,38.97&amp;year=1889">What was here in 1889? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
